00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:35.880
But not, like, maybe not.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.560
Maybe we're just thinking of Final Fantasy VI.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.260
Yeah, there you go. Yeah, Terra there as well.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.920
Or Tina, depending on which version of the game you play.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.620
But this game, you know, even though it's a shmup, has a pretty cool plot.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:55.840
It makes a point of letting you know right off the bat that it takes place in a century on the space calendar.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.840
Now, that's very vague, right?

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:01.800
What is a century on the space calendar?

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:05.340
Is that a unit of time that's longer than our centuries?

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:09.500
Or is it just that the space calendar, like, has more months than hours?

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:14.060
Feels confusing to put those two things together.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:19.240
A century is something we measure time in, but a space calendar is not something we track time on.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:26.540
So, but real quick, I'm just going to say I did not read the manual for this game, so everything you say is going to be new to me.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:27.880
This is going to be fantastic.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:53.640
If I can extrapolate some things about culture for a bit, I will say that in the 70s and the 80s, I don't think screenwriters, writers, and I guess video game writers, and specifically video game manual writers, really knew how to write about space.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:05:00.720
You got people using a measure of distance as a measure of time in Star Wars.

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:09.180
You got all the crazy wild shit that we've seen in these other video game manuals that make zero sense.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:14.540
So I think that's just another symptom of that.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:16.880
They don't know what that means.

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:18.920
Okay, I like it.

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:25.120
I can boogie with that, I suppose, because that's not even, that's not plot oriented.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.660
That's just a confusing way to start a sentence.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:37.280
Yes. But the rest of the plot is basically this space demon king whose name is Mandler brought a bunch of his monsters.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:38.580
They make a point to say monsters.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:40.380
Again, sorry for the sidetrack here.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.020
Not aliens. Yeah, they're monsters.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:47.360
So is it like, did the space demon king come from space hell?

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:50.020
Like, is there, does each planet have its own hell?

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:51.660
See, that's also strange.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:55.640
Like, now they're mixing the sci-fi with the fantasy, and I'm confused.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:11.460
I also, from not having any information on the plot of this game, thought maybe you were traveling back in time because there are obvious dinosaurs scattered along the levels with turrets in the background.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:29.980
So I didn't know if it was like a Jurassic Park situation, or if it was like a Land of the Lost situation, or Dino Crisis, or just some random alien world that you were on that just so happened to have alien dinosaur-alikes.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:31.920
So maybe you have the answer.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:37.560
Yeah, it's just a century so far ahead in the space calendar that basically we did dinosaurs again.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:39.740
Yeah, none of that makes any sense.

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:51.380
And so the space demon king and his monsters come to Earth, and basically the Earthlings, that's what they call them, I'm not, you know, the person who wrote this manual is an alien.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:56.560
The Earthlings are forced to go live at the bottom of the ocean.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:02.600
Not like something that would be feasible to do, right, unless they were preparing for it in advance, right?

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:06.580
That would take a long time to be like, all right, well, these guys are showing up.

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:08.100
Let's go hide in the ocean.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:09.720
It doesn't seem like a good idea.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:11.040
But that's what they do.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:18.260
They hide in the ocean, and it says in the manual that they've been blessed by sunshine.

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:34.880
And the Earth Recovery Army Terra Cresta, so that's where Terra Cresta comes from, it's the name of the army, sends its combat interceptor, the Wing Galibur, to recapture the parts left on the hangers from above ground.

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:41.100
So they brought the Galibur underground into the bottom of the ocean, but they didn't bring the rest of it.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:44.440
That's confusing because they took the time to build the infrastructure down there.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:53.140
But now the Wing Galibur is back up, and it's going to collect the other parts and defeat the evil space demon king, Mandler.

00:07:53.140 --> 00:08:05.440
The only problem with any of this is that if you played the game like Sean did, and like I'm sure some of you did, without reading the manual, you wouldn't know any of this was happening, and you wouldn't care.

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:06.680
You wouldn't give a fuck.

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:23.260
Right, Sean? Yeah, the only thing that gives any indication of the fate of humanity at the beginning of this game is the some, again, if you haven't read the manual, it's just this weird decision that your ship comes out of the ocean.

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:30.020
And I was like, oh, that's cool, I wonder what that means, and then immediately forgot as I was playing.

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:34.940
But yeah, no, this is all just happening in your imagination.

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:49.660
Right, and is it just like, is the whole thing devised to be really cool and get you intrigued if you, you know, are either on the car ride home or just reading through your manuals, and now you're like, oh, I didn't know that was the plot, let me go check out this game again.

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:54.760
Or is it just a way to try to be clever about why you're picking up the other parts?

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:56.520
Because I never thought about it when I was playing the game.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:58.460
I never thought like, why are these parts here?

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:00.720
Like, it's early video games, I don't give a shit.

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:03.280
Like, I'm just happy my vehicle is cooler.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:13.060
I know that we've talked about this in the past, but I think it's important to note that in games like these, you're not asking why.

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:17.540
There's no reason to, because the answer is always because it's cool.

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:25.280
And don't tell me why the parts are on land and not explain why dinosaurs are back.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:29.360
Yeah, absolutely.

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:47.440
Last week I said we weren't going to talk about a bunch of things that we've already covered on beat-em-ups, and I feel like we owe the same kindness to our listeners for this shmup episode, where we should just dive right into arguably the most interesting part of this game, and that is the power-up system.

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:52.000
That is what makes Terra Cresta different than other shmups we have played.

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:59.440
The way it works is, yes, you are just doing a shmup where enemies will appear on screen in waves.

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:01.720
You have to take them out and make sure you survive.

00:10:02.020 --> 00:10:09.640
And then eventually, if you survive long enough, you'll see these kind of capsules on the ground that have the number on them between one and five.

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:24.740
And once the player shoots all the ones with the number one capsule down, it's basically like a hangar opens up where another piece of the wing galibur, but basically, think of it also like another ship, comes out.

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:30.000
And you fly into it, collect it, and now your ship is upgraded.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:36.420
So it's like, you know, a little larger, but also shoots a couple different, like, projectiles now.

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:42.320
And it also acts as a shield because it gives the player one extra hit before they die.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:52.940
So if you take any kind of bullet damage or run into an enemy, your additional pieces that you've acquired go away, not the galibur, which is always great news.

00:10:52.940 --> 00:11:00.540
Because obviously, the same is not true in an essential game like Life Force, where, you know, one hit always kills you regardless.

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:15.240
Now, the interesting thing on top of all of that is that should the player add all the pieces to the ship and press the transform button on your controller, the wing galibur will transform into an invulnerable...

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:19.360
A burning... Yeah, an invincible, burning, soaring phoenix.

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:22.380
And it's one of the coolest things ever.

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:25.120
You couldn't have said it better, Sean.

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:33.360
It's like, now we have literally transformed into a live, like, I refuse to believe that that's the ship on fire.

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:35.080
Like, that is an actual bird now.

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:36.500
We have become the phoenix.

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:43.680
And through the powers of something, we are now, like, invincible and able to just wreck everything.

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:45.140
It's pretty great.

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:53.120
So, this game, you know, with everything you just described, it was different levels of...

00:11:53.120 --> 00:12:01.780
It happens very rarely now, where I'm playing one of these games, and I'm like, I genuinely think to myself, like, oh shit, that was cool.

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:03.860
And not, like, neat.

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:07.040
Or something much more measured.

00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:23.460
But the fact that with its upgrades, the pieces of the ship are now visibly pieces on your ship, and it's not just some weird, like, color change, or it's not even reflected, or it's just something, like, circling you.

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:25.220
Like, it's an added part of your ship.

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:26.220
Your sprite gets bigger.

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:27.980
It's harder to maneuver around things.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:32.040
It has meaningful effects on the gameplay, as well as looking cool.

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:47.800
Then, once you press that transform button, which Mike kind of just mentioned, but that's a whole other part of the gameplay, where you can then break off the parts of your ship that you picked up, like a zord.

00:12:49.540 --> 00:13:05.600
And then, now you're fighting sort of independently as, like, a squadron of ships, and the trajectories that the different pieces are shooting in are kind of all at different angles, so you've got, like, a wider spread.

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:19.460
And you're only going to take damage if your original ship gets hit, so you can use that to sort of maneuver, if you have a transform token, or whatever you want to call it, to maneuver more bullet-hel-y areas.

00:13:20.140 --> 00:13:21.940
And it just looks awesome.

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:26.240
And then, once you got the whole ship, you turned into the phoenix.

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:28.860
It just, like, was really cool.

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:30.260
All of that was so cool.

00:13:30.260 --> 00:13:37.280
And say that wasn't enough features for you in terms of how the power-up system works in this game.

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:40.020
Remember, we've always said shmups are always about their power-ups.

00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:53.800
If it's not cool enough that you collect pieces of the ship to get a better ship, and then you can also send out all of the pieces of your ship to kind of have this spread of ships where only your main ship can be damaged,

00:13:53.800 --> 00:14:07.740
there's a other menu mode in this game called formation design where you can go in and customize where you prefer each piece of your ship to be and what direction you prefer them to shoot at,

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:11.880
so this way you can think about the kind of spreads that you want in your game.

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:15.500
It lets you customize how the power-ups work.

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:23.360
And to me, you put any of those features together, and I think you've got an interesting shmup, but now I'm, like, over the edge.

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:32.000
Like, wow, they really thought of a lot when it comes to player control over the only thing that you do in this game, shoot.

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:34.320
You know, like, you have so many ways to shoot.

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:37.320
You have so many different options for how you want to handle shooting.

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:43.060
You get more than one life, theoretically, as long as you always have another piece of your ship.

00:14:43.060 --> 00:14:48.700
And you have the ability to kind of say, like, you know, nah, screw it, actually.

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:56.700
I want more ships to cover more ground, but I'm willing to take that chance of, like, the one-hit KO as a result.

00:14:56.900 --> 00:15:03.940
Yeah, I really love that risk-reward of using the transform mode because, yeah, you don't...

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:08.340
Because there's also a shield that you can get so you'd keep your full ship,

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:18.000
but then you won't have the shield behind you before you get to that penultimate hit where you just get broken down to the original hit.

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:21.740
But if you do transform, all of those safety nets get ripped away.

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:30.240
And I think there's just a lot of decision-making going on as the player when you start to, like, think about all this, you know?

00:15:31.220 --> 00:15:39.140
One, you might really hate losing any pieces of your ship that you collect because, keep mind, when you get hit, they go away.

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:44.580
So it really stings because you have to collect all of them to get that awesome Phoenix thing.

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:49.020
So it's great that it feels good to have them all and it's not just some, like,

00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:53.320
yep, now you're the fully decked-out version of your ship and, you know, your spread's really large.

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:59.460
It's an actual invincible form that you know is incredibly useful in a shmup like this.

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:01.420
There's a lot of...

00:16:03.880 --> 00:16:07.840
It's a mixture of caution and excitement at the same time, if that makes sense.

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:10.520
Because it's very exciting to be a powerful ship.

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:13.240
It feels bad to go back to the base form.

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:19.620
But they give the pieces of the ship out frequently enough that it's also not a total reset

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:25.140
like we've seen in other shmups where, like, I was in the middle of this stage,

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:28.800
had all these awesome power-ups, now I'm back to the base ship

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:33.000
and good luck getting any of my power-ups back before I die again.

00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:37.300
And it is a hard game, I will admit that.

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:41.240
But it also, yeah, that whole frequency of upgrade.

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:48.200
And, yes, it's a linear sort of builds on each other like a Gradius,

00:16:48.340 --> 00:16:53.780
but there's less steps and they're more frequent.

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:58.100
You know what I think makes it a hard game more than, like, just bullets?

00:16:58.340 --> 00:17:01.920
Like, bullets are gonna be in any game and they always suck when there's, like,

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:03.340
too many enemies or whatever.

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:06.440
But I actually think the hardest thing about this game is something that

00:17:06.440 --> 00:17:09.680
maybe could be learned with, like, enough playthroughs.

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:14.040
But I actually find the enemy patterns, like, the way that the ships move,

00:17:14.140 --> 00:17:19.180
they move a lot around the middle and center of the screen,

00:17:19.200 --> 00:17:21.820
an area that isn't ever a safe zone, right?

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:23.900
You usually either hang back or move up.

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:28.900
These guys move a lot in the center and find ways to keep looping back around

00:17:28.900 --> 00:17:29.960
to get in that area.

00:17:29.980 --> 00:17:34.260
And so I just find myself being stuck a lot, you know, like,

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:37.240
being in a position where I'm like, well, shit, now I'm in the corner

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:38.820
and bullets are flying at me.

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:42.680
Yeah, if you get in the corner, you're kind of fucked, yeah. Right, so it's, like, it's something that potentially could be learned.

00:17:42.860 --> 00:17:45.640
But, yeah, that's what I found to be the most challenging is that some of the

00:17:45.640 --> 00:17:51.180
enemy patterns are maybe a little more vicious than we see in the usual, like,

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:55.940
here's a straight line of enemies or here's enemies that come in from the side.

00:17:56.060 --> 00:17:59.480
It's like, no, some of these enemies, like, they all come down in different

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:03.560
directions and then they all, like, loop back in a giant circle and then they

00:18:03.560 --> 00:18:05.640
fire all these beams in different directions.

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:07.100
And it's like, oh, God, no.

00:18:07.780 --> 00:18:16.100
And then when you're in a boss fight, I think it's a bit more infrequent that these

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:20.540
formations sort of come, but you're still dealing with waves of enemies while these

00:18:20.540 --> 00:18:21.580
boss fights are happening.

00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:24.380
So it definitely piles on.

00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:30.700
And the boss fights are sticking to the trope that I guess started with Gradius, or if it

00:18:30.700 --> 00:18:33.900
didn't start with Gradius, forgive me, but at least it started on this podcast with Gradius

00:18:33.900 --> 00:18:39.720
of just the, you know, shoot the core style boss fight where you also just have to manage

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:45.500
everything else. But those guys aren't, you know, necessarily a cakewalk either.

00:18:45.700 --> 00:18:51.420
Like, the way that they shoot their bullets winds up becoming more of an interesting, like,

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:54.540
figure eight spread and stuff like that as the game goes on.

00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:59.580
And so those aren't very easy because the only, they're shooting nonstop and you have to shoot

